1296:
The board of commissioners finds that signs provide an important medium through which individuals may convey a variety of non-commercial and commercial messages. However, left completely unregulated, signs can become a threat to public safety as a traffic hazard and detriment to property values and to Lumpkin County's overall public welfare as an aesthetic nuisance. By enacting this chapter, the board of commissioners intends to:
A. Balance the rights of individuals to convey their messages through signs and the right of the public to be protected against the unrestricted proliferation of signs;
B. Further the objectives of the Lumpkin County Comprehensive Plan;
C. Protect the public health, safety, and welfare;
D. Reduce traffic and pedestrian hazards;
E. Maintain and enhance the visual environment, and preserve the right of the citizens to enjoy Lumpkin County's scenic beauty;
F. Protect property values by minimizing possible adverse effects and visual blight caused by signs;
G. Avoid the harmful aspects of the unrestricted proliferation of signs;
H. Promote economic development; and
I. Ensure the fair and consistent enforcement of sign regulations.
(Res. No. 2017-66, exh. A, 9-19-2017; Res. No. 2017-71, exh. A, 10-17-2017)

