No user shall contribute or cause to be contributed, directly of indirectly, into the authority's wastewater system the following pollutants, substances, or wastewater:
(1) Any liquids, solids or gases which by reason of their nature or quantity are, or may be, sufficient either alone or by interaction with other substances to cause fire or explosion or be injurious in any other way to the authority's wastewater system or to the operation of the authority's wastewater system. At no time shall a wastestream be discharged with a closed-cup flashpoint of less than 140 degrees Fahrenheit (60 degrees Celsius) using the test methods specified in 40 CFR 261.21. At no time shall a wastestream be discharged which yields two successive readings on an explosion hazard meter, at the point of discharge into the system (or at any point in the system), of more than five percent, nor any single reading over ten percent, of the lower explosive limit (LEL) of the meter;
(2) Wastewater having a pH less than 6.0 or more than 9.5, or otherwise causing corrosive structural damage to the authority's wastewater system or equipment or wastewater having any other corrosive property capable of causing damage or hazard to structures, equipment and/or personnel of the authority's wastewater system, unless the authority's wastewater system is specifically designed to accommodate such wastewater;
(3) Solid or viscous substances in amounts which will cause obstruction of the flow in the sewer system or authority's wastewater system resulting in interference including, but not limited to, garbage with particles greater than one-half inch in any dimension, ashes, cinders, animal entrails, paunch, manure, offal, bones, hair, hide, hides or fleshings, whole blood, beer or distillery slops, feathers, sand, lime residues, cannery waste, stone or marble dust, metal, grass straw, grass clippings, rags, spent grains, spent hops, wastepaper, wood, plastics, paper, cardboard, fiberglass, paint or ink residues, gas, tar, asphalt residues, chemical residues, solid grease of animal or mineral origin, or residues from refining or processing wastes;
(4) Pollutants, including oxygen-demanding pollutants (BOD, etc.), released in a discharge at a flowrate and/or pollutant concentration which, either singly or by interaction with other pollutants, will cause interference with the authority's wastewater system. In no case shall a discharge have a flow rate or contain concentration or quantities of pollutants that exceed for any time period longer than 15 minutes, more than five times the average 24-hour concentration, quantities, or flow during normal operation of the discharger's facility;
(5) Wastewater having a temperature greater than 90 degrees Fahrenheit (32 degrees Celsius) or less than 59 degrees Fahrenheit (15 degrees Celsius), or which will inhibit biological activity in the treatment plant resulting in interference or impermissible pass through;
(6) Petroleum oil, nonbiodegradable cutting oil, or products of mineral oil origin, in amounts that will cause interference or impermissible pass through;
(7) Pollutants which result in the presence of toxic gases, vapors, or fumes within the authority's wastewater system, either singly or by interaction with other pollutants, in a quantity that may cause acute worker health and safety problems or are sufficient to create a public nuisance as defined by state law or as may be determined by a court with applicable jurisdiction;
(8) Any wastewater containing toxic pollutants in sufficient quantity, either singly or by interaction with other pollutants, to injure or interfere with any wastewater treatment process, constitute a hazard to humans or animals, create a toxic effect in the receiving waters of the authority's wastewater system, or to exceed the limitation set forth in a federal categorical pretreatment standard. A toxic pollutant shall include, but not be limited to, any pollutant identified pursuant to section 307(a) of the Act;
(9) Trucked or hauled pollutants, except at discharge points designated by the director in accordance with this article. No septic tank or other trucked or hauled pollutants will be permitted, except by special license and by special agreement, to include the approval of the agreement by any applicable treatment plant;
(10) Noxious or malodorous liquids, gases, solids, or other wastewater which, either singly or by interaction with other wastes, are sufficient to create a public annoyance or danger or a hazard to life, or to prevent entry into the sewers for maintenance or repair;
(11) The discharge of any wastewater, sewage, or polluted water to any natural outlet or onto any public or private property shall be prohibited;
(12) Wastewater which imparts color which cannot be removed by the treatment process, such as, but not limited to, dye wastes and vegetable tanning solutions, which consequently imparts color to a treatment plant's effluent;
(13) Wastewater containing any radioactive wastes or isotopes except in compliance with applicable state or federal regulations;
(14) Stormwater, surface water, groundwater, artesian well water, roof runoff, subsurface drainage, swimming pool drainage, condensate, deionized water, noncontact cooling water, and unpolluted wastewater shall not be discharged through direct or indirect connection to a sanitary sewer, unless specifically authorized by the director. Under no conditions will sanitary sewage be discharged to a storm sewer;
(15) Sludge, screenings, or other residues from the pretreatment of industrial wastes;
(16) Any substance which may cause the authority's wastewater system effluent or any other product of the authority's wastewater system such as residues, sludges, or scums, to be unsuitable for reclamation and reuse or to interfere with the reclamation process where the authority's wastewater system is pursuing a reuse and reclamation program. In no case shall a substance discharged to the authority's wastewater system be in noncompliance with sludge use or disposal criteria, guidelines, or regulations developed under section 405 of the Act, any criteria, guidelines, or regulations affecting sludge use or disposal developed pursuant to the Solid Waste Disposal Act, the Clean Air Act, the Toxic Substances Control Act, state criteria applicable to the sludge management method being used, or any future regulation;
(17) Medical wastes, except as specifically authorized by the director in a permit;
(18) Wastewater causing, alone or in conjunction with other sources, a treatment plant's effluent to fail toxicity test;
(19) Detergents, surface-active agents, or other substances which may cause excessive foaming in the authority's wastewater system;
(20) Fats, oils, or greases of animal or vegetable origin in concentrations greater than 100 milligrams per liter. Fats, oils, and greases shall not exceed 20 percent of BODs by weight;
(21) Any substance which will cause the authority's wastewater system to violate any applicable NPDES, LAS permit, any other applicable permit, the receiving water quality standards, and/or any other applicable federal, state, or local law, rule or regulation.
(Res. No. 2005-55, art. II(2), 9-15-2005)

