(a) Generally. In the case of any discharge, including, but not limited to, accidental discharges, discharges of a nonroutine, episodic nature, a noncustomary batch discharge, or slug load, that may cause potential problems for the authority's wastewater system, it is the responsibility of the user to immediately telephone and notify the authority of the incident. The notification shall include:
(1) Location of discharge;
(2) Type of waste discharged;
(3) Concentration and volume; and
(4) Corrective actions taken by the user.
(b) Written notice. Within five calendar days following an accidental discharge, the user shall submit to the authority a detailed written report describing the cause of the discharge and the measures to be taken by the user to prevent similar future occurrences. Such notification shall not relieve the user of any expense, loss, damage, or other liability which may be incurred as a result of damage to the authority's wastewater system, fish kills, or any other damage to person or property; nor shall such notification relieve the user of any fines, civil penalties, or other liability which may be imposed by this section or other applicable law. Noncompliance with this provision shall constitute a violation of this section and subject the user to the penalties described in this section.
(c) Notice to employees. A notice shall be permanently posted on the user's bulletin board or other prominent place advising employees to call the authority in the event of a discharge of any of the substances enumerated in this article as a prohibited discharge.
(d) Accidental discharge/slug control plan. Periodically, the director shall evaluate whether each significant industrial user needs an accidental discharge/slug control plan. In addition, the director may require any user to develop, submit for approval, and implement such a plan. Alternatively, the director may develop such a plan for any user. An accidental discharge slug control plan shall address, at a minimum, the following:
(1) Description of discharge practices, including nonroutine batch discharges;
(2) Description of stored chemicals;
(3) Procedures for immediately notifying the director of any accidental or slug discharge; and
(4) Procedures to prevent adverse impact from any accidental or slug discharge.
(Res. No. 2005-55, art. II(6), 9-15-2005)

